## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of closures, promises, and memoization in a practical frontend pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Closures and encapsulation
- In-flight deduping of promises
- TTL caching and cleanup
- ES6 features: Map, arrow functions, async/await
- Testing approaches for async caches

## Code Example
```javascript
function createDataLoader(fetchFn) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const TTL = 60_000;
  const now = () => Date.now();
  function cleanup() {
    const t = now();
    for (const [k, v] of cache) {
      if (t - v.ts >= TTL) cache.delete(k);
    }
  }
  setInterval(cleanup, TTL);
  return {
    load(id) {
      const entry = cache.get(id);
      if (entry && (now() - entry.ts) < TTL) return entry.promise;
      const p = Promise.resolve(fetchFn(id)).finally(() => {});
      cache.set(id, { promise: p, ts: now() });
      return p;
    },
    clear(id){ cache.delete(id); },
    clearAll(){ cache.clear(); }
  };
}

// usage
const loader = createDataLoader((id)=> fetch(`/api/user/${id}`).then(r=>r.json()));
loader.load(123).then(console.log);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test TTL expiry and concurrent loads?
- How would you adapt for per-resource invalidation or gc tuning?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design performance-sensitive React architectures handling high-frequency data, including state shaping, rendering discipline, and UX consistency under load.

## Key Concepts

- Memoized selectors and reselect
- Data virtualization (react-window) for large series
- Rendering optimizations with React.memo and useSelector shallowEqual
- Concurrent UI patterns with useTransition
- Context isolation to prevent unnecessary rerenders

## Code Example

```javascript
import { createSelector } from 'reselect';

// Input: state.metrics.stream = Array<{t: number, v: number}>
const bucketSelector = (state) => state.metrics.stream;

export const selectLast1sBuckets = createSelector(
  bucketSelector,
  (stream) => {
    const now = Date.now();
    const oneSec = 1000;
    const buckets = {};
    for (const p of stream) {
      const bucket = Math.floor((now - p.t) / oneSec);
      buckets[bucket] = (buckets[bucket] || 0) + p.v;
    }
    // return as ordered array of {k,v}
    return Object.keys(buckets).sort((a,b)=>a-b).map(k=>({t: Number(k), v: buckets[k]}));
  }
);
```

